**Proposition 16.2 (resource and clock correspondence).** These recurrences
specify a complete opcode/purpose vector on the one actual installation
and service run. If its scalar sum through cut $z$ is $C(z)$, then the
actual ticks, fee and storage are

$$
T(z)=C(z),\qquad F(z)=2C(z),\qquad
S(z)=S_0+b_{\rm ctl}(z)+8A(z)+8C(z).
\tag{RCA.11}
$$

$A$ includes all old source and work cells, bank versions, scratch,
manifest, descriptors, archive, mailbox and receiver cells. $C$ includes
all their receipt constructions. None of these coordinates is a logical
selector-width optimum. A target's unary word is acquired computation
or input, never a mathematical resource allowance.

Proof. Definition 11.5 reduces every compiler instruction to the displayed
primitive graphs; Lemma 13.2a includes both manifest-entry moves, all
field/terminal tests and the exact emitted fields. §§13–14 reduce ingress,
installation, source adaptation, outputs and return to those graphs and
existing primitive supplier paths.
There is no remaining instruction that reads a string, updates a map,
allocates a block, calls a decoder or restores an entry in one step.
Assign each primitive one entering purpose tag and retain the tag through
its helpers, counting each explicit tag-changing CTL. Add costs at their
actual successor heads, rather than adding maxima from separate runs.
PR57.D1 gives one receipt/tick and two fee units per occurrence. Each
ordinary EX constructs one eight-bit cell, including replaced R and new
R accounting by net one cell; installed finite control is counted in its
own actual bit size. Induction on the expanded graph gives (RCA.11) at
all interior cuts, including partially initialized destinations and banks.
This proves a symbolic executable price; it asserts no numerical full-run
measurement and no physical optimum. ∎


